.TH CHTTPSVR_SUBROUTER 3 "2026-08-03" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_subrouter \- create a sub-router with a path prefix
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

chttpsvr_router *chttpsvr_subrouter(chttpsvr srv, const char *prefix);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a new router under the path prefix that you give, and attaches
it to the server.  That prefix must start with \&'/'.  A route that you
register on the sub-router matches against the part of the path after
the prefix.  The sub-router owns every path under the prefix; see
NOTES.

The prefix must not have two slashes together, for example "//api" or
"/a//b".  Such a prefix can never match a valid HTTP path, and it
would silently create a router that nothing can reach.
chttpsvr_subrouter therefore rejects it and returns NULL.

The library removes a slash at the end of the prefix automatically.
"/api/v1/" and "/api/v1" are therefore the same, and they give the
same routing behaviour.

The server owns the pointer that this function gives you, and the
caller must not free it.  Every chttpsvr_router_on,
chttpsvr_router_on_stream and chttpsvr_router_use call on that router
first checks that nothing destroyed this server.  That check also
covers a concurrent destroy from another thread.  It is therefore safe
to keep that pointer across a restart with chttpsvr_stop and
chttpsvr_start.  A registration call that races a concurrent
chttpsvr_destroy of srv returns ccol_invalid_args, and touches no
freed memory.

This function is thread-safe.  You can call it before the server
starts, and after it starts.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A mounted prefix owns every path under it.  A path is under a prefix
when its decoded form, the one that chttpsvr_req_path reports, equals
the prefix or continues it with a '/'.  "/admin", "/admin/x/y",
"/%61dmin/x" and "/admin%2Fx" are all under "/admin"; "/administrator"
is not.  The most specific mount that owns the path handles the
request alone, so a mount of "/api/v1" wins over a mount of "/api" for
"/api/v1/x".  No route of a less specific mount, and no root-level
route, can answer it.  When none of the routes of the owner matches,
the owner answers 404, or 405 when a route of the owner matches the
path and not the method, and the middleware of the owner runs for
that rejection as it runs for its routes.  A path under no mount goes
to the root router, which holds the routes that
chttpsvr_register_handler and chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler
register directly on the server.

A guard in the middleware of a sub-router therefore covers every path
under its prefix, whatever any other router registers.  A root-level
pattern such as "/{a}/{b}/{c}" overlaps every three-segment path, yet
it never answers "/admin/a/b" while "/admin" is mounted.  A mount
nested under another one, such as "/admin/deep" under "/admin", owns
the paths under itself, so the middleware of "/admin" does not run for
them; give the nested mount a guard of its own when it needs one.  Two
sub-routers on the identical prefix share it: the routes of both can
answer, the earlier registration first, and the earlier one answers a
404 or a 405.  Precedence therefore depends only on the prefixes and
the patterns that you register, and never on the order of
registration.  Inside one router, the server tries the routes in
registration order.  See chttpsvr_register_handler(3).

The server decodes each raw segment on its own before it matches it.
"/admin%2Fx" is under "/admin", but no route of that mount can match
it, because a route matches whole segments, so the mount answers it
with a 404.

The "/" prefix is a special case.  The loop that strips a slash at the
end runs only for a prefix longer than one character.  The library
therefore keeps "/" as it is, with prefix_len = 1.  A "/" sub-router
owns only the exact request path "/".  No other path is under it, and
the server routes every other path as if the "/" sub-router did not
exist.  This includes a path that starts with a second slash, for
example "//foo".  Every route on a "/" sub-router is therefore
reachable only from the exact path "/".

That makes this prefix a trap for a sub-router that carries its own
middleware.  An authenticator or a rate limiter on a "/" sub-router
does not refuse everything outside "/".  A root-level route, or
another sub-router, serves any other path normally, and the middleware
of the "/" sub-router does not run for it.

Use the prefix "/" only when you want to match the exact root path "/"
and nothing else.  Register a middleware globally with chttpsvr_use
when you want it to run for every request, whatever the path.
Register routes directly on the server with chttpsvr_register_handler
or chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler when you want to catch every
request, whatever the path.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A pointer to the new chttpsvr_router on success.  It gives NULL when
srv is CHTTPSVR_INVALID, when srv is a stale handle that something
already destroyed, when prefix is NULL, when prefix does not start
with '/', when prefix has two slashes together, or when the library
runs out of memory.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_router_on (3),
.BR chttpsvr_router_use (3)
